  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "Leon Tamman was a Sudan-born businessman and philanthropist who established a pharmaceutical company and invested in Israeli businesses and luxury hotels. His family traded successfully in Sudan before moving to Egypt, which they later had to leave. Based in Britain, he became a leading figure in international Sephardi communal organisations.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 91,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "First-person memoir and contemporary institutional reporting support the business and community outline."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S2",
      "S3",
      "S5"
    ],
    "word_count": 50,
    "html": "<p>Leon Tamman was a Sudan-born businessman and philanthropist who established a pharmaceutical company and invested in Israeli businesses and luxury hotels. His family traded successfully in Sudan before moving to Egypt, which they later had to leave. Based in Britain, he became a leading figure in international Sephardi communal organisations.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "At a World Jewish Congress assembly in Jerusalem, Leon Tamman offered support for the Israeli Black Panthers' welfare work if the group renounced violence. He met its leader, Shalom Cohen, and hosted members at dinner to agree the arrangement. Tamman then announced a personal scholarship gift for children in the city's Musrarah neighbourhood.\n\nHis support for education also reached academic research. The Leon Tamman Foundation sponsored Bar-Ilan University's Sociological Institute for Community Studies, which examined Jewish identity, migration and relations between communities. It also supported the institute's journal, Sociological Papers, published in English to reach readers beyond Israel.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 93,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Contemporary reporting describes the personal negotiation and scholarship gift. The university directly documents institutional and journal support."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S5",
      "S6"
    ],
    "word_count": 98,
    "html": "<p>At a World Jewish Congress assembly in Jerusalem, Leon Tamman offered support for the Israeli Black Panthers' welfare work if the group renounced violence. He met its leader, Shalom Cohen, and hosted members at dinner to agree the arrangement. Tamman then announced a personal scholarship gift for children in the city's Musrarah neighbourhood.</p>\r\n<p>His support for education also reached academic research. The Leon Tamman Foundation sponsored Bar-Ilan University's Sociological Institute for Community Studies, which examined Jewish identity, migration and relations between communities. It also supported the institute's journal, Sociological Papers, published in English to reach readers beyond Israel.</p>\r\n"
  },
